---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="An abstract map schematic in flat gold and black shapes on warm cream — diagonal lanes, diamond objective markers and scattered ward dots, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will be given match footage, drafts and game states — sometimes your
    own, sometimes a stranger's — and asked to explain them: not what
    happened, but why the winning side won. Across the semester you build a
    vocabulary for that explanation — resources, wave states, information,
    tempo, objectives, composition, teamfighting — and each assessment asks
    you to use it on evidence you can point to, not on how the game felt to
    watch. The artefact you walk out with is the ability to trace a causal
    chain from an opening draft to a Nexus falling, in a single match you've
    never seen before.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who already knows the basic rules of League of Legends — lanes,
    champions, items, objectives, what ends the game — and wants to argue
    about it with more precision than "he's just built different." This is
    not a climbing guide, a champion guide or a patch-summary service, and it
    won't teach you mechanics. <strong>Rank is not evidence</strong> here: no
    minimum rank is required to enrol, and no rank, in an assessment, counts
    as an argument. What is marked is whether a claim survives being asked
    "how do you know?"
  </p>

  <h2>Why this isn't a pile of guides</h2>
  <p>
    A guide tells you what to do in a recurring situation. This course asks a
    different question — given a game state, what actually explains why one
    side's decisions converted into a win and the other's didn't — and that
    question doesn't have a lookup-table answer. Champion examples show up
    throughout the semester, but only ever to illustrate a principle; they
    never replace it, and the same reasoning is meant to survive the next
    patch, the next meta, and a champion you've never played.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The complete twelve-week teaching schedule, Week 1 to Week 12.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        Two supplementary in-person workshops early in the semester, alongside
        the weekly lectures.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
